# Line endings. One file, two audiences, and the order below matters:
# a later rule wins, so the general case comes first and the exceptions after.

# PRODUCT CODE - normalised to LF, in the repository and in the working copy.
#
# This project is built on Windows, macOS and Linux, and the same tag has to
# produce the same bytes on all three. The machine used here has a global
# core.autocrlf=true, which would otherwise hand each platform a different
# working copy and make "reproducible build" quietly untrue.
* text=auto eol=lf

# ONE TEST FILE MUST KEEP THE BYTES THIS REPOSITORY OTHERWISE NORMALISES AWAY.
#
# The pack format forbids the two-character line ending, and the format test
# suite is required to hold a file breaking every rule. Under the setting above
# that file would be converted on checkout and would keep passing while testing
# nothing - a guard that is green and blind, which is the exact failure this
# product exists to find elsewhere.
#
# Measured on 2026-09-06, not assumed: the byte order mark survives `text=auto`
# untouched, so the file covering that rule needs no exception and does not have
# one. Only the line ending is rewritten, so only this single path is listed.
#
# Deliberately one path and not a pattern. If a second such file ever appears it
# gets its own line, so that nothing else in the suite quietly stops being
# normalised alongside it.
/tests/packs/rejected/crlf-line-endings.toml -text

# THE SHIPPED TYPEFACE - the upstream bytes, unaltered.
#
# `text=auto` above would almost certainly leave a .ttf alone, because Git
# detects binary content by looking for NUL bytes. Almost certainly is not the
# standard this file already holds itself to: the exception below it exists
# because a guard that is green and blind is the failure this product hunts
# elsewhere. A font mangled by line-ending conversion does not fail loudly, it
# fails as a face that will not load, on someone else's machine.
#
# `binary` is `-text -diff`: no conversion, and no attempt to diff it either.
/crates/nkb-gui/ui/fonts/** binary

# THE APPLICATION ICON - the bytes the generator wrote, unaltered.
#
# An .ico is binary through and through: nine pictures behind a directory of
# offsets. `text=auto` would leave it alone only because it happens to contain a
# NUL byte, and an icon with a mangled offset does not fail at the build - it
# fails as a program with a blank icon on someone else's desktop. The two SVG
# files beside it are text and stay normalised to LF like the rest.
/crates/nkb-gui/assets/*.ico binary

# THE VENDORED UNICODE DATA - the upstream bytes, unaltered.
#
# These files are already LF-only, so `text=auto eol=lf` above would convert
# nothing. They are listed anyway, for the same reason the typeface above is:
# what makes the bytes safe today is a property of the current content, not a
# rule, and the next release of the Unicode Character Database is not under our
# control. A checksum in that directory's README names the exact bytes taken
# from unicode.org, and a conversion on checkout would quietly make it false.
/crates/nkb-core/unicode/** -text

# PROJECT MEMORY - no conversion at all.
#
# These documents contain escaped invisible characters and examples where every
# byte counts. Measured, not assumed: with conversion left on, a fresh clone came
# back with different bytes in four files.
#
# Deliberately narrow. This must never impose "no normalisation" on source code,
# which is the separate decision made above.
/CLAUDE.md      -text
/.gitignore     -text
/.gitattributes -text
docs/**         -text
tools/**        -text
